Building Detection — Orthomosaic + DSM Height Filtering
Overview
Built a geospatial building-detection pipeline that finds building footprints from drone RGB orthomosaics (GeoTIFF) and uses elevation data to eliminate flat false positives. Outputs georeferenced GeoJSON polygons (loadable in QGIS) with per-building area and height.

Model Used
LangSAM = Segment Anything (SAM) + GroundingDINO — a zero-shot, text-prompted instance segmentation model. I prompt it with "building roof" (box threshold 0.24, text threshold 0.20), so it segments rooftops directly without any custom training. (detect_buildings.py)

Pipeline
Block-wise processing — the orthomosaic is too large to fit in memory/GPU, so it's split into 2048 × 2048 blocks with 200 px overlap (rasterio windowed reads).
Pre-filtering of blocks — skips empty/black blocks, very dark (shadow) blocks, and mostly-green blocks (>70% vegetation) since forests have no buildings — saves GPU time.
Mask false-positive filtering (is_building_mask) — rejects: full-block masks (SAM over-segmentation), area outside 20–2000 m², masks that are >55% green (vegetation), and uses rectangularity (buildings are rectangular) as a shape cue.
Per-class NMS (IoU 0.3) — removes duplicate detections from overlapping blocks.
Real-world geometry — pixel area converted to m² using the raster CRS/resolution (handles both projected CRS and WGS84 lat/lon).
The DSM Height Filter (key accuracy step)
LangSAM, working on RGB alone, mistakes flat surfaces — roads, bare fields, concrete pads, bare soil — for buildings. To remove these I filter every detection against the DSM (Digital Surface Model) (filter_and_add_dsm_heights):

Ground level = 10th percentile of the DSM in the surrounding area (the local terrain reference — effectively the DTM under the building).
Roof level = 85th percentile of the DSM inside the footprint.
Height = roof − ground (this is the normalized height / nDSM concept, DSM − ground/DTM).
A detection is kept only if ≥40% of its pixels rise ≥5 m above ground, and is rejected if it's large-but-low. Uses median height to stay robust to water tanks/parapets.
Handles CRS mismatch between ortho and DSM via on-the-fly rasterio reprojection.
This turns a 2D RGB detector into a true 3D-aware building detector — flat ground gets dropped, only objects with real vertical structure survive.

Output
GeoJSON FeatureCollection of building polygons with area_m2, max_height_m, mean_height_m, roof_m, ground_m, and center lat/lon; plus total building count and total footprint area.

Tech Stack
Python · SAM + GroundingDINO (LangSAM / samgeo) · PyTorch (CUDA) · rasterio (GeoTIFF I/O, windowing, CRS reprojection) · OpenCV (contours, shape filters, NMS) · NumPy · GeoJSON.

Tree Detection — DeepForest + DSM/DTM (CHM) Filtering
Overview
Built an individual-tree-detection pipeline that finds single tree crowns from drone orthomosaics and uses elevation data to separate real trees from grass/lawn/shrubs — the hard problem in rural aerial surveys, since grass is green but not a tree. Outputs georeferenced GeoJSON with crown area and tree height.

Model Used
DeepForest (weecology/deepforest-tree) — a RetinaNet-based deep-learning object detector pretrained on NEON airborne imagery specifically for tree-crown bounding-box detection. (detect_trees.py)

Pipeline
GSD-aware resampling — DeepForest is trained around ~10 cm/pixel; I resample the ortho to a target ~15 cm GSD, tuned for dense Indian tropical canopies so the model detects whole crowns instead of fragmenting one tree into many sub-crowns.
Tiled inference — predict_tile with 400 px patches, 15% overlap; detections are scaled back to native resolution.
Score + crown-area filtering — keep score ≥ 0.3 and crown area 2–500 m² (drops noise and unrealistic blobs).
Green color filter (HSV) (filter_green_only) — removes non-green false positives (vehicles, roofs, rocks); trees are green, those aren't.
Crown merging — merges overlapping/touching boxes into a single crown so one tree = one detection.
NMS (IoU 0.3) — final dedup across tile seams.
The DSM − DTM Height Filter (the "avoid grass" step)
The green filter keeps trees and grass (both green). To remove grass, I compute each detection's height above ground using DSM and DTM (add_dsm_heights) — this is the Canopy Height Model (CHM = DSM − DTM):

Canopy top = max DSM value at the crown center (the top of the tree surface).
Ground level = the DTM value at that location (true bare-earth elevation). If no DTM is supplied, ground is estimated from the 10th percentile of the surrounding DSM.
Tree height = canopy_top − ground = DSM − DTM.
If height ≥ 1.5 m → real tree; if below 1.5 m → "greenland"/grass and discarded.
Grass and lawns are green and flat — they pass the color filter but fail the height filter (DSM ≈ DTM) — so this cleanly isolates actual trees from ground vegetation. Handles ortho↔DSM↔DTM CRS reprojection.

Output
GeoJSON of tree crowns with score, crown_area_m2, height_m, canopy_top_m, ground_m, center coordinates; plus total tree count and total crown area.

Tech Stack
Python · DeepForest (RetinaNet, NEON-pretrained) · PyTorch (CUDA) · rasterio (GeoTIFF, DSM/DTM sampling, CRS reprojection) · OpenCV (HSV filter, box merge, NMS) · NumPy · GeoJSON.

